    "Informed Consent for Ambient Documentation Using Generative AI in Ambulatory Care. JAMA Netw Open. 2025. Observational quality-improvement study at NYU Langone Health, March-December 2024; 121 participants (18 ambulatory clinicians, 103 patients). 74.8% of patients reported comfort with ambient documentation; 81.6% consented when given basic information, falling to 55.3% when given detailed information about AI features, data storage and corporate involvement. Comfort varied by encounter type: 63.1% for routine physical examination, 40.8% for sexual health, 35.0% for mental health. Over 98% rated knowing where audio was sent and who could access recordings as important. The authors recommend opt-in consent with a flexible multimodal approach including previsit notification, in-visit confirmation, nonclinical staff support and opt-out options.": {"doi":"10.1001/jamanetworkopen.2025.22400","url":"https://doi.org/10.1001/jamanetworkopen.2025.22400","verified_by":"pubmed-and-doi","checked_at":"2026-08-30"},
    "Institute for Clinical and Economic Review. Cost-Effectiveness, the QALY, and the evLYG; 2023 Value Assessment Framework. ICER uses the equal value life year gained as its usual measure of health gain, weighting years of life gained uniformly regardless of age, disease or level of disability in order to address the potential discriminatory effect of the cost per QALY, while also reporting cost per QALY for benchmarking against other assessments.": {"url":"https://icer.org/our-approach/methods-process/cost-effectiveness-the-qaly-and-the-evlyg/","verified_by":"official-icer-page","checked_at":"2026-08-30"},
